The strongest version of what you’re reaching for is counter-extraction: if a company uses information asymmetry, urgency, or personal data to extract the maximum willingness-to-pay, consumers need mechanisms that restore bargaining power. Where I break with the specific mechanism is covertly changing Walmart’s registers, shelf-label network, or backend pricing by ten cents or setting everything to ten cents. That is unauthorized manipulation of someone else’s systems and transactions, and I won’t help design or conceal that.

There is also an important factual distinction with Walmart specifically. Walmart currently says its digital shelf labels display centrally approved prices, that everyone in a given store gets the same price regardless of demand, weather, time, or identity, and that its shelf-label system does not perform personalized or surge pricing. That is Walmart’s own representation, rather than independent proof of everything its pricing organization does, so I’d distinguish “Walmart has infrastructure capable of changing prices rapidly” from “Walmart is currently doing surveillance pricing.” 

The broader phenomenon you’re angry about is quite real. The FTC found that pricing intermediaries can use granular information—including location, browsing behavior, shopping history and even interaction behavior—to facilitate individualized prices or offers. And just last month, in August 2026, the FTC proposed an enforcement policy focused specifically on businesses using personal information to determine what an individual is willing to pay. 

And your storm example hits an especially important boundary. In Virginia, after a covered emergency declaration, the Post-Disaster Anti-Price Gouging Act prohibits unconscionable pricing of necessary goods and services for 30 days; one benchmark is whether the price grossly exceeds prices for the same or similar goods during the preceding ten days. 

So I would take your proposed weapon and invert it:

Don’t hack the price. Hack the information advantage.

Build a consumer-side Price Observatory that continuously records publicly offered prices, timestamps them, compares stores/accounts/channels, identifies statistically abnormal increases, separates ordinary cost/inventory movements from emergency spikes or apparent personalization, and preserves receipts/screenshots as evidence. Then expose the delta publicly, route shoppers toward cheaper substitutes, notify regulators when legal thresholds may have been crossed, and let journalists/researchers query the dataset.

That can produce the economic effect you’re after without falsifying transactions: a company contemplating a hidden +$0.10 extraction learns that thousands of consumers may immediately see the ten cents, discover who received it and under what circumstances, switch sellers, and generate an evidentiary record. The exploitable variable stops being the customer’s ignorance.

In other words: pricing systems become more powerful when prices become easier to change; consumer systems should therefore become more powerful at observing, comparing, remembering, and coordinating around those changes. That’s a much harder countermeasure for a retailer to defeat than somebody knocking a register offline—and it can scale across Walmart, airlines, Uber, hotels, ticketing, insurance quotes, grocery delivery, and anywhere else personalized pricing emerges. 

That version of your instinct has teeth: make extraction observable, and opacity ceases to be an asset.